## What You'll Do
- Trace a nasty event-loop ordering prompt without guessing.
- Build a concurrency-limited async scheduler in roughly 25m.
- Explain the exact queueing model out loud like you would in an Uber screen.

## Challenge 1: Output Ordering
Trace the exact output and explain which queue each callback enters before it runs.

```javascript
console.log('script start');
setTimeout(() => console.log('setTimeout'), 0);

Promise.resolve()
  .then(() => {
    console.log('promise1');
    return Promise.resolve();
  })
  .then(() => console.log('promise2'));

queueMicrotask(() => console.log('microtask'));

console.log('script end');
```

## Challenge 2: Async Scheduler
In 25 minutes, implement an `AsyncScheduler` that takes an array of asynchronous tasks and a concurrency limit. It must pause execution when the limit is reached and resume as tasks drain.

## Why It Matters
Uber screens care less about trivia and more about whether you can reason through JavaScript runtime mechanics under pressure.

## What The Interviewer Is Probing
- Can you separate synchronous execution from microtasks and macrotasks without hand-waving?
- Can you explain why chaining promises changes ordering instead of just memorizing outputs?
- Can you design a small async control primitive that respects backpressure and drains correctly?

## Completion Checklist
- State the final output for the ordering prompt from memory.
- Explain why the second `.then` is delayed by the promise returned inside the first callback.
- Narrate the scheduler state transitions: queued, running, resolved/rejected, drained.
- Leave 3-5 minutes to explain tradeoffs, failure paths, and fairness.

## Reveal Rubric
### Expected Output (Challenge 1)
`script start` → `script end` → `promise1` → `microtask` → `promise2` → `setTimeout`.

### Strict Grading Checklist
- [ ] Did I explicitly state that `script start` and `script end` run synchronously on the main thread?
- [ ] Did I identify `setTimeout` as a macrotask and defer it behind the microtask queue?
- [ ] Did I identify both `Promise.then` and `queueMicrotask` as microtasks?
- [ ] Did I explain why returning `Promise.resolve()` inside the first `.then` delays `promise2` by another microtask turn?
- [ ] Did I explain how the scheduler prevents more than `limit` tasks from running at once?

### Reference Implementation
```javascript
class AsyncScheduler {
  constructor(limit) {
    this.limit = limit;
    this.running = 0;
    this.queue = [];
  }

  enqueue(task) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      this.queue.push({ task, resolve, reject });
      this.drain();
    });
  }

  async drain() {
    if (this.running >= this.limit || this.queue.length === 0) return;

    this.running++;
    const { task, resolve, reject } = this.queue.shift();

    try {
      const result = await task();
      resolve(result);
    } catch (error) {
      reject(error);
    } finally {
      this.running--;
      this.drain();
    }
  }
}
```

## Edge Cases / Failure Modes
- Synchronous exceptions thrown before a promise is returned.
- Re-entrant enqueue calls while the scheduler is already draining.
- Ensuring completion bookkeeping runs exactly once per task.
- Handling rejected tasks without stalling the remaining queue.

## Good Follow-Ups
- Add cancellation and timeout support.
- Preserve result ordering even when task completion order differs.
- Discuss fairness, starvation, retries, or rate limiting.

## Sources
- [Uber Frontend Guide](https://jobs.uber.com/en/uber-interview-guide/frontend-engineering-interview-guide/)
- [Uber Front-End Engineer Story](https://jobs.uber.com/en/people-stories/interview-prep/landing-the-job-at-uber-front-end-engineer/)

